/// A Char widens to a String, and an Integer to a Double.
///
/// Turbo Pascal's assignment compatibility, which the language follows: a
/// conversion that loses nothing and cannot fail happens without being asked,
/// and never runs backwards.  Both processors implement no widening at all --
/// every line below is rejected by the checker before the program runs.
///
/// ⚠️ The asymmetry that makes this more than a convenience: a Char argument is
/// ALREADY accepted for a String parameter, so the language has the conversion
/// and only the declaration path refuses it.  Passing 'A' to a String parameter
/// works today; declaring 'var S : String := ''A''' does not.
///
/// Narrowing stays illegal and is asserted nowhere here.  A String does not
/// become a Char even when it holds one code point, and a Double does not become
/// an Integer even when it is whole.
///
/// Fails with: Type mismatch!  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
test 'A Char widens to a String and an Integer to a Double';
begin
    var Text  : String := 'A';
    var Ratio : Double := 1;

    AssertEqual (1, Length (Text));
    AssertTrue  (Text is String);
    AssertEqual (1.0, Ratio);
end
